---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About | Sign Language Translator"
  description="About SignLanguage-Translator.com — a free American Sign Language learning aid built by Firoz Khan of FK Digital Media."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900">About</h1>

    <div class="prose-lg mt-6 space-y-5 text-slate-700">
      <p>
        <strong>SignLanguage-Translator.com</strong> is a free learning aid for
        American Sign Language (ASL). Type English text and see it broken into
        ASL signs — word-level signs from our dictionary where available, and
        fingerspelling for everything else.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is built and maintained by <strong>Firoz Khan</strong> of{" "}
        <strong>FK Digital Media</strong>, with one goal: make the first steps
        of learning ASL accessible to anyone with a browser.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What this site is</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>A vocabulary learning aid for ASL beginners</li>
        <li>A text-to-sign sequence translator with playback controls</li>
        <li>A growing dictionary of common ASL words and phrases</li>
        <li>An A–Z and 0–9 fingerspelling reference</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What this site is not</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          <strong>Not a substitute for a qualified interpreter.</strong> For
          medical, legal, emergency, or other important communication, always
          use a certified ASL interpreter.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a grammar teacher.</strong> ASL has its own grammar and
          word order, which differs from English. This tool shows vocabulary
          sign by sign; it does not rearrange sentences into ASL grammar.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a certification.</strong> Text descriptions are a starting
          point. Real fluency comes from learning with Deaf instructors and the
          Deaf community.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="rounded-xl border-l-4 border-amber-500 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3">
        We describe things honestly here: what the site can do, what it cannot
        do, and where human instruction is irreplaceable.
      </p>

      <h2 class="pt-4 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How sign descriptions are written</h2>
      <p>
        Every dictionary entry is a text-based, step-by-step guide researched from
        established ASL references — including Lifeprint, university teaching
        materials, and public-health resources — and rewritten in our own original
        words. We deliberately do not use AI-generated hand images: a wrong or
        distorted handshape could teach a sign incorrectly. Commissioned photos
        and videos from qualified signers are the planned next upgrade for this
        dictionary.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
